Retry secret metadata writes that conflict with a concurrent write

Three methods of `SecretUserMetadatumRepo` in
barbican/model/repositories.py write the user metadata of a secret:
`create_replace_user_metadata` runs `DELETE ... WHERE secret_id = ?`
and inserts the new pairs, `create_replace_user_metadatum` deletes one
`(secret_id, key)` and inserts it, and `delete_metadatum` deletes one
`(secret_id, key)`. All run in the one transaction of the request,
which `BarbicanTransactionHook` (a pecan `TransactionHook`,
barbican/api/hooks.py) commits after the controller returns. None of
them is retried, and a write that loses against a concurrent request
fails in one of two ways:

- New secrets, MySQL error 1213. Under REPEATABLE READ a DELETE that
  finds no row locks the gap before the next record of the unique
  index `_secret_key_uc (secret_id, key)`. Gap locks of two
  transactions do not conflict, so both DELETEs succeed. Each INSERT
  then needs an insert-intention lock in that gap and waits for the
  gap lock of the other transaction, and InnoDB rolls one of them
  back. With random UUIDs the chance that two requests share a gap
  falls as the table grows and never reaches zero.
- One secret, MariaDB error 1020 ("Record has changed since last
  read"). The request reads the secret first, which starts its
  snapshot. MariaDB 12.3.3, which a fresh database of CobaltCore
  OpenStack runs since the mariadb-operator chart 26.10.2
  (C5C3/cobaltcore#1243), has innodb_snapshot_isolation=ON, and a
  DELETE that meets a row another transaction committed after that
  snapshot fails instead of waiting and overwriting.

oslo.db (18.0.0 for 2026.1, 18.1.1 for 2026.2) raises `DBDeadlock` for
error 1213 and its subclass `DBConsistencyError` for error 1020. The
error reaches `controllers.handle_exceptions`, which answers 500, for
PUT /v1/secrets/{id}/metadata with "Secret metadata creation failure
seen - please contact site administrator.".

On 2026-11-15 in the C5C3/playground cluster (release 2026.1, three
Barbican API replicas, MariaDB 12.3.3 Galera with three members, every
Barbican write on one member since C5C3/cobaltcore#1702), 2 of 197,531
metadata PUTs of Dizzy's barbican-monitor answered 500 between 15:25
and 18:25 UTC. `Innodb_deadlocks` on the writer stood at 3, matching
the 3 HTTP 500 that Dizzy counted on metadata updates. The LATEST
DETECTED DEADLOCK of the writer shows two INSERTs into
secret_user_metadata for two new secrets, each waiting for an
insert-intention lock in a gap of `_secret_key_uc` that the other
transaction held.

Measured on 2026-11-15 in a Docker rig: mariadb:12.3.3 (one node,
REPEATABLE-READ and innodb_snapshot_isolation=ON, the server defaults)
and the published images ghcr.io/c5c3/barbican:2026.1 (barbican 22.0.0,
oslo.db 18.0.0) and ghcr.io/c5c3/barbican:2026.2 (barbican 23.0.0,
oslo.db 18.1.1). In each of 20 rounds 8 threads start at once, and each
does what one API request does: read the secret, call one repository
method, commit. That is 160 calls per mode; the rig and the probe are
in C5C3/cobaltcore#1752. Failed calls of 160 without the patch:

  mode              2026.1   2026.2   errors
  new                    6        4   1213
  new-metadatum          8        4   1213
  same                 140      112   1020, some 1213
  same-metadatum       140      109   1020, some 1213
  same-delete          109       69   1020, some 1213

The modes new and new-metadatum call create_replace_user_metadata and
create_replace_user_metadatum on one new secret per thread, same and
same-metadatum call them with all threads on one secret, and in
same-delete four threads replace two pairs of one secret while four
call delete_metadatum on one of them. With the patch 0 of 160 calls
fail in each mode on both images, every secret of the first four modes
ends with exactly one row, and the slowest call takes 0.81 s. With 32
threads 0 of 640 calls fail in each mode on both images. With the
retry on the two replace methods only, 45 of 160 calls of same-delete
still fail on 2026.1, all with error 1020.

The patch adds `retry_on_write_conflict` and
`WRITE_CONFLICT_MAX_RETRIES = 10` to barbican/model/repositories.py and
puts the decorator on the three methods, whose bodies and signatures
do not change. It is oslo_db.api.wrap_db_retry(retry_on_deadlock=True)
around a function that, on `DBDeadlock`, rolls the session back, which
ends the transaction and its snapshot, logs a WARNING and re-raises,
so that the method runs again in a new transaction. InnoDB has rolled
back the whole transaction of the request when the error arrives, so
the DELETE and the INSERTs have to run again. The retry sits on the
repository methods, not on the controllers: a retry of a controller
method would run inside `handle_exceptions`, which turns every
exception into a 500, and would repeat body parsing and validation.

The first wait is 0.05 s, each later one a random time below a bound
that doubles from 0.1 s and is capped at 1 s, so ten retries wait
0.05 + 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.4 + 0.8 + 5 x 1 = 6.55 s at most. These are the
intervals of keystone patch 0004 (C5C3/cobaltcore#1739). The default
first wait of oslo.db is 1 s, and the barbican-operator runs 2 uWSGI
processes with 1 thread per pod by default, so a waiting request
blocks half a pod, while the winner of a conflict commits within
milliseconds. The count and the intervals are constants, as in
barbican patch 0002 and keystone patch 0004.

Each lost attempt logs one WARNING, "<Class.method> lost against a
concurrent write (<error class>) and is run again". It names the
method and the error class and leaves out the exception text, which
carries the SQL statement with the metadata values. Keystone 0004 logs
its retries at DEBUG, because a collision there is the routine of the
first logins of a day; here 3 collisions happened in 197,531 calls,
and barbican patch 0002 logs a WARNING for the same event. When the
retries run out, oslo.db logs "DB exceeded retry limit." with the
traceback at ERROR and re-raises, `handle_exceptions` answers 500 as
before, and the rolled-back session stores nothing of the request.
`DBConnectionError`, `DBDuplicateEntry`, `DBReferenceError` and every
other exception are not retried and leave without a rollback in the
decorator; the `TransactionHook` rolls back as before. `DBDeadlock`
also covers MySQL error 1205 (lock wait timeout). With the defaults
of the barbican-operator it does not occur on this path: the socket
timeouts read_timeout=10 and write_timeout=10 of the database
connection end the wait after 10 s with a `DBConnectionError`.

The rollback discards every uncommitted change of the session, and
only the decorated method runs again, so the decorator fits only a
method whose callers have written nothing earlier in the request. The
three methods have four call sites, all in
barbican/api/controllers/secretmeta.py:
`SecretMetadataController.on_put` (PUT /v1/secrets/{id}/metadata),
`SecretMetadataController.on_post` (POST /v1/secrets/{id}/metadata),
`SecretMetadatumController.on_put`
(PUT /v1/secrets/{id}/metadata/{key}) and
`SecretMetadatumController.on_delete`
(DELETE /v1/secrets/{id}/metadata/{key}). Before the repository call
each request only reads: the secret in `SecretsController._lookup`,
its ACLs and project in `ACLMixin.get_acl_tuple`, and the metadata in
`on_post` and in `SecretMetadatumController.on_put`. The rollback
expires the secret the controller holds, and SQLAlchemy loads it again
in the new transaction when the response reads `self.secret.id`. The
409 of `on_post` (key already present) and the 404 of
`SecretMetadatumController.on_put` (key absent) stay decided on the
snapshot of the first attempt, as for a request without a conflict,
whose DELETE also acts on rows committed after its check. A retried
replace writes its pairs over what the winner committed, so the last
writer wins, and a retried `delete_metadatum` that finds the key gone
deletes nothing, so the request answers 204, as for an absent key.

The patch does not prevent the conflict: the deadlock still happens
and `Innodb_deadlocks` still counts it, and the patch runs the write
again. A conflict that surfaces at the COMMIT of the request, outside
the three methods, is not retried.

Tests: no upstream test moves. No test in
barbican/tests/model/repositories/test_repositories_secret_metadata.py
or barbican/tests/api/controllers/test_secretmeta.py asserts that a
conflict escapes. The patch adds to
`WhenTestingSecretMetadataRepository` the attribute `new_metadata`, the
helper `_create_secret_with_metadata` and the context manager
`_write_conflicts`. That context manager fails
the first attempts of a method after their first INSERT or their
DELETE ran in the session, records each wait of the retry instead of
sleeping, calls an optional `winner` during the wait, the way the
request that won the conflict commits first, and records the row count
of each DELETE. It adds fifteen tests:

- test_replace_user_metadata_retries_a_deadlock, which also pins the
  arguments of the WARNING,
- test_create_user_metadata_retries_a_deadlock (a secret without
  metadata, the case of two requests in one gap),
- test_replace_user_metadata_with_no_pairs_retries_a_conflict,
- test_replace_user_metadata_retries_a_changed_record
  (`DBConsistencyError`),
- test_replace_user_metadata_retries_a_conflict_on_its_delete,
- test_replace_user_metadata_replaces_a_concurrent_replace (the last
  writer wins),
- test_replace_user_metadata_raises_after_the_last_retry (11 attempts,
  waits of 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.4 and 0.8 s and five of 1 s when every
  random wait takes the top of its range, the metadata unchanged),
- test_replace_user_metadata_does_not_retry, once each for
  `DBConnectionError`, `DBDuplicateEntry` and `DBReferenceError` (one
  attempt, no wait, no WARNING),
- test_replace_user_metadatum_retries_a_deadlock,
- test_replace_user_metadatum_retries_a_changed_record
  (`DBConsistencyError` on the DELETE),
- test_create_user_metadatum_retries_a_deadlock,
- test_replace_user_metadatum_raises_after_the_last_retry,
- test_delete_user_metadatum_retries_a_changed_record,
- test_delete_user_metadatum_another_request_deleted_first (the retry
  finds no row and raises nothing),
- test_delete_user_metadatum_raises_after_the_last_retry.

To barbican/tests/api/controllers/test_secretmeta.py the patch adds
the class `WhenTestingSecretMetadataWriteConflicts` with one test per
call site: test_replace_metadata_after_a_lost_write (PUT
/v1/secrets/{id}/metadata, 201 with the `metadata_ref` of the secret),
test_create_metadatum_after_a_lost_write (POST, 201 with the
`Location` of the key), test_update_metadatum_after_a_lost_write (PUT
/v1/secrets/{id}/metadata/{key}, 200) and
test_delete_metadatum_after_a_lost_write (DELETE, 204). Each sends its
request while the first INSERT or DELETE of the repository method
fails once, and asserts the response and the stored metadata. Each
also asserts that the request ran no INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE and left
no change pending in its session before it called the method, the
condition the rollback of the retry depends on.

With only the test hunks applied, on 22.0.0 and on 23.0.0, all but the
three cases of test_replace_user_metadata_does_not_retry fail. With
the rollback removed from the decorator, the three tests that raise
after the last retry and
test_delete_user_metadatum_another_request_deleted_first fail, because
the DELETE of a failed attempt stays in the session.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_barbican.sh (Test 11) makes the
first INSERT or DELETE of each of the three methods raise `DBDeadlock`
in the built image and asserts that the method runs again and stores
the metadata.

The three patched files carry the same blobs in barbican 22.0.0 and
23.0.0, and the twins under patches/barbican/2026.1/ and
patches/barbican/2026.2/ differ only in their Applies-to line.

Applies to barbican 22.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

